1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,829
This is a simple race day routine for inline speed skaters.

2
00:00:03,829 --> 00:00:10,321
You will learn what to check in race week, the night before, and in the last hour before your start.

3
00:00:10,321 --> 00:00:15,773
Most skaters train hard for months, then lose time through preparation, not fitness.

4
00:00:15,773 --> 00:00:17,980
A bolt works loose in the warm up.

5
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:20,707
The wheel choice is wrong for the surface.

6
00:00:20,707 --> 00:00:24,731
The bearings were stripped the night before and never settled.

7
00:00:24,731 --> 00:00:26,354
None of this is dramatic.

8
00:00:26,354 --> 00:00:31,093
But every one of these costs you on race day, and every one is avoidable.

9
00:00:31,793 --> 00:00:34,560
One rule runs through this whole routine.

10
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,529
Race day is not the day to try anything new.

11
00:00:37,529 --> 00:00:42,253
Everything you race on should be something you have already skated on.

12
00:00:42,253 --> 00:00:45,695
Preparation is about making a known setup reliable.

13
00:00:45,695 --> 00:00:48,125
It is not about making it different.

14
00:00:48,125 --> 00:00:52,174
So the plan follows the way a race weekend actually unfolds.

15
00:00:52,174 --> 00:00:56,831
There is race week, the night before, and the hour before your start.

16
00:00:56,831 --> 00:01:00,542
The idea is to push every decision as early as you can.

17
00:01:00,542 --> 00:01:04,794
That way race day is only about checking, never about choosing.

18
00:01:05,494 --> 00:01:08,000
Here is the whole routine on one line.

19
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,397
In race week, you confirm your wheel choice, clean and oil your bearings, and skate on the setup.

20
00:01:14,397 --> 00:01:20,596
The night before, you check every bolt, rotate wheels if they need it, and pack the bag fully.

21
00:01:20,596 --> 00:01:28,378
In the hour before, you spin each wheel and listen, feel the actual surface, and get your suit on with your laces set.

22
00:01:28,378 --> 00:01:30,620
And one line sits under all of it.

23
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:34,115
Nothing new goes on the skate after race week closes.

24
00:01:34,815 --> 00:01:37,947
The most common mistake in race week is change.

25
00:01:37,947 --> 00:01:42,945
New wheels, new bearings, a new frame position, a suit you have never worn.

26
00:01:42,945 --> 00:01:46,276
Each one adds an unknown right when you want none.

27
00:01:46,276 --> 00:01:52,606
If something really needs replacing, do it early, so you get two or three sessions on it first.

28
00:01:52,606 --> 00:01:54,072
Here is a simple test.

29
00:01:54,072 --> 00:01:59,403
Change something only if it is worn out, broken, or clearly wrong for the venue.

30
00:01:59,403 --> 00:02:06,066
A wheel with a chunk out of it, a bearing that grinds, a frayed lace or a cracked strap all qualify.

31
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:09,797
Leave it alone if the only reason to touch it is nerves.

32
00:02:09,797 --> 00:02:16,194
Frame position, boot heat moulding and your lacing pattern take sessions to adapt to, not hours.

33
00:02:16,194 --> 00:02:20,391
Bearings that spin freely and sound clean can stay as they are.

34
00:02:21,091 --> 00:02:26,413
Wheel firmness is the one setting that really should change with the venue.

35
00:02:26,413 --> 00:02:29,464
Many skaters think harder is always faster.

36
00:02:29,464 --> 00:02:32,870
On a rough or dusty surface, that is often true.

37
00:02:32,870 --> 00:02:41,597
But on a hard, smooth, dry track, a wheel that is too hard starts to slide instead of holding your line through the corner.

38
00:02:41,597 --> 00:02:45,571
And slip costs far more than a small difference in roll.

39
00:02:45,571 --> 00:02:47,628
This diagram shows the trade.

40
00:02:47,628 --> 00:02:53,801
An XFirm wheel, at 84 to 86A, is softer and grips better on hard, smooth, dry surfaces.

41
00:02:53,801 --> 00:03:00,400
An XXFirm wheel, at 86 to 88A, gives more roll and outright speed when the surface allows it.

42
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,593
Dust, damp and tight corners all favour grip.

43
00:03:03,593 --> 00:03:05,508
Long straights favour roll.

44
00:03:05,508 --> 00:03:14,165
And if you are unsure, race the wheel you trained on, because confidence in the corner is worth more than a gain on paper.

45
00:03:14,865 --> 00:03:17,655
Bearings are the classic race day mistake.

46
00:03:17,655 --> 00:03:20,976
Skaters strip and oil a full set the night before.

47
00:03:20,976 --> 00:03:29,611
Then the wheels feel heavy and noisy for the first few laps, because nothing has bedded in and there is too much oil in the races.

48
00:03:29,611 --> 00:03:35,523
Service them early in race week instead, and skate on them at least once before you race.

49
00:03:35,523 --> 00:03:41,169
If they are dirty, clean them and let them dry completely, because oil binds to grit.

50
00:03:41,169 --> 00:03:47,280
Then add one drop of oil per bearing, spin it by hand to spread it, and wipe off the excess.

51
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:49,073
One drop is the sweet spot.

52
00:03:49,073 --> 00:03:51,863
A flooded bearing collects dust and drags.

53
00:03:51,863 --> 00:03:53,524
And remember the numbers.

54
00:03:53,524 --> 00:04:00,099
A pair of four wheel skates runs sixteen bearings, so a spare or two in the bag is cheap insurance.

55
00:04:00,799 --> 00:04:04,691
The night before is a mechanical job, not a performance one.

56
00:04:04,691 --> 00:04:06,831
Work through each skate in order.

57
00:04:06,831 --> 00:04:11,436
Check that every axle is tight and that no wheel has side to side play.

58
00:04:11,436 --> 00:04:17,144
Check the frame mounting bolts on both skates, because these loosen slowly and silently.

59
00:04:17,144 --> 00:04:18,895
Spin each wheel and listen.

60
00:04:18,895 --> 00:04:23,500
A rough or rattly bearing should tell you tonight, not during the race.

61
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:28,819
Look across the wheels for uneven wear, and rotate if one is clearly further gone.

62
00:04:28,819 --> 00:04:33,035
Then check your laces, buckles and straps for fraying and cracks.

63
00:04:33,035 --> 00:04:36,602
Snug each frame bolt firmly without leaning on the key.

64
00:04:36,602 --> 00:04:41,077
And if you moved your frame recently, confirm it is where you expect.

65
00:04:41,077 --> 00:04:47,174
Frame position drift is easy to miss on the bench, and you feel it straight away off the line.

66
00:04:47,874 --> 00:04:51,265
Pack the bag the night before, not in the morning.

67
00:04:51,265 --> 00:04:55,672
A checklist beats memory when you are nervous and short of sleep.

68
00:04:55,672 --> 00:04:58,317
This layout splits it into four groups.

69
00:04:58,317 --> 00:05:04,826
Skate kit: your skates with race wheels fitted, a second wheel set, your helmet and spare laces.

70
00:05:04,826 --> 00:05:10,861
Tools and spares: allen keys, bearing oil, spare bearings, spare axles and a clean cloth.

71
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:16,828
Race clothing: your skin suit, warm layers, spare socks, gloves and a dry change of kit.

72
00:05:16,828 --> 00:05:23,609
And fuel and admin: water, simple food, race numbers, your licence and ID, and the start list times.

73
00:05:23,609 --> 00:05:27,949
Check it against the list once in the morning, and you are done.

74
00:05:28,649 --> 00:05:33,955
Your race suit belongs on the list too, because it is equipment, not just clothing.

75
00:05:33,955 --> 00:05:37,982
It should be dry, uncreased, and already familiar on your body.

76
00:05:37,982 --> 00:05:44,375
A suit that binds across the shoulders or rides at the knee changes how you sit in the low position.

77
00:05:44,375 --> 00:05:50,128
So wear it in training before you race in it, and never open a new suit on the start line.

78
00:05:50,128 --> 00:05:55,754
Put it on before your final skate checks, so you are not fighting a zip with cold hands.

79
00:05:55,754 --> 00:05:58,886
Have a warm layer to pull over it while you wait.

80
00:05:58,886 --> 00:06:04,512
And carry a dry change for after the race, so you spend the next week training, not ill.

81
00:06:05,211 --> 00:06:08,160
By the last hour, nothing should need fixing.

82
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:11,895
This hour is for confirming, warming up and staying calm.

83
00:06:11,895 --> 00:06:18,972
At sixty minutes, spin each wheel and listen, and check the axles and frame bolts after the trip in the bag.

84
00:06:18,972 --> 00:06:28,473
At forty five minutes, do an easy skate on the race surface and read the grip, so your first contact with it is not the first corner of the race.

85
00:06:28,473 --> 00:06:35,616
At twenty five minutes, get your suit on with a warm layer over it, and set your laces the way you always do.

86
00:06:35,616 --> 00:06:40,333
At fifteen minutes, do your activation and short efforts, and sip water.

87
00:06:40,333 --> 00:06:43,741
Then put the tools away, breathe, and race the plan.

88
00:06:43,741 --> 00:06:49,638
If you are still holding an allen key at fifteen minutes, something went wrong last night.

89
00:06:50,338 --> 00:06:56,111
If you race more than one distance, keep the routine between heats short and repeatable.

90
00:06:56,111 --> 00:06:59,391
Wipe the grit off the wheels, keep warm and drink.

91
00:06:59,391 --> 00:07:03,065
Only touch the skates if something has actually changed.

92
00:07:03,065 --> 00:07:06,410
Fiddling between rounds is almost always a mistake.

93
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:09,887
After the race, take ten minutes to protect your kit.

94
00:07:09,887 --> 00:07:14,742
Wipe the wheels and frames down, especially after a dusty or damp surface.

95
00:07:14,742 --> 00:07:18,218
Look for any wheel that took a chunk out in the pack.

96
00:07:18,218 --> 00:07:23,926
Loosen the laces, let the boots air, and do not leave a damp suit balled up in the bag.

97
00:07:23,926 --> 00:07:29,633
If your bearings sounded gritty, note it, and service them early in the next race week.

98
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:32,967
If you remember one thing, make it this.

99
00:07:32,967 --> 00:07:36,457
Nothing new goes on the skate after race week closes.

100
00:07:36,457 --> 00:07:42,580
Decide early, check late, and let the last fifteen minutes belong to your legs and your head.

101
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:49,428
The single most overlooked check is the frame mounting bolts, so check them the night before every race.

102
00:07:49,428 --> 00:07:56,145
The full guide, with the complete checklists and answers to common questions, is on the LiveLica blog.

103
00:07:56,145 --> 00:07:58,186
The link is in the description.
